;; M4S named fallback (docs/m4s-contract.md): a lattice INSIDE the struct
;; cone.  Both heads read `in`, so any edit's cone holds the struct
;; constructor and the lattice writer together; lattices anywhere in the
;; cone stay refused by name under M4S (M6L/M7 own those shapes), and the
;; struct relation keeps the route outside M6L's certificate -- the flush
;; must settle through clear-and-rerun from both directions.
;;
;; With in = {(1,5), (1,7), (2,3)}:
;;   out   = { (pair 1 5) (pair 1 7) (pair 2 3) }
;;   score = { 1 -> 7, 2 -> 3 }          (max lattice)
;; Deleting in(1,7): out loses (pair 1 7) (2 live ids + 1 tombstone) and
;; score's key 1 must REGRESS to 5 -- only a rerun can lower a lattice
;; payload today.

struct (pair int int)
lattice (best (max int))
table (in int int)
table (out pair)
table (score int best)

rule
(in X Y)
-->
(out (pair X Y))

rule
(in X Y)
-->
(score X Y)
